          "secNumber": "1.1",
          "heading": "Curriculum Framework & Foundations",
          "content": "Phase transformations connect thermodynamic possibility, atomic mobility, microstructural geometry and processing history. This book is the sixth draft textbook in the OpenSTEM Materials Science & Engineering sequence. Earlier books introduce structure, diffraction, materials thermodynamics, diffusion and mechanical behavior; here these subjects meet in concrete pathways from one state to another. The same alloy composition can produce drastically different products when cooled slowly, quenched and tempered, or held isothermally, because a phase diagram tells us *which states are favored in equilibrium* but not *how quickly a structure can change*. The central discipline is to keep that distinction visible throughout any calculation.\n\nOur coverage proceeds from free-energy curves and chemical potentials through nucleation, interface motion, isothermal and non-isothermal kinetics, solidification, ferrite-pearlite-bainite-martensite pathways in steels, precipitation and coarsening, ordering and glass transition, and finally experimental design and process qualification. Every teaching model is deliberately simplified and every tabulated exercise dataset is generated by an equation rather than a physical measurement. No individual recipe may be used to qualify a real component. The reader should carry forward a habit: label the phase state, the mechanism, the time-temperature path, the transport constraints, the observation technique and the model validity domain each time a transformation is discussed.",
