          "secNumber": "1.6",
          "heading": "Lesson 1.1: Phases, components and structural identity",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nA phase is a region with uniform intensive properties and structural description at a scale of observation. A change from face-centered-cubic to body-centered-cubic symmetry can be a phase transformation even if composition is unchanged. In two-phase alloys the matrix and precipitate have distinguishable lattice structures, compositions or orders. Grain boundaries within one phase are not separate phases merely because orientations differ.\n\n**Working relationship.** Phase identification requires structural evidence plus independent chemical or thermodynamic context. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **phases, components and structural identity**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nPrepare a fictitious binary coupon at two annealing temperatures; match diffraction peaks to candidate lattices, use an elemental map to distinguish chemical partitioning, and state whether the data indicate a phase transition or only grain growth.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Lever-rule product fraction** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Bulk solute fraction = 0.2428571 fraction**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **0.1547619 fraction**. At **Bulk solute fraction = 0.3257143 fraction**, the response is **0.29285714 fraction**; within this idealization it increases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **1.6666667 fraction per fraction**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nTwo overlapping diffraction patterns may result from texture, retained surface scale or instrumental doublets; a micrograph alone cannot always establish phase identity.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** How would you test whether a weak new peak is a second phase or a diffraction artifact? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
          "simulations": []
