          "secNumber": "1.7",
          "heading": "Lesson 1.2: Gibbs energy, stability and metastability",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nAt fixed pressure and temperature stable equilibrium minimizes an appropriate Gibbs energy subject to overall mass conservation. A metastable state can persist when a barrier separates local and global minima. The sign of a bulk free-energy difference supplies a driving force but does not directly specify transformation speed. If phases carry different compositions, chemical potentials\u2014not simply plotted energy heights\u2014govern exchange.\n\n**Working relationship.** For equal composition under specified T,p, a lower molar G indicates bulk equilibrium preference; kinetics requires an additional model. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **gibbs energy, stability and metastability**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nCompare invented molar Gibbs energies of two polymorphs differing by 260 J/mol at 750 K. Calculate the preference under a shared composition and then ask whether a nucleation barrier could prevent the favored crystal from appearing on cooling.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Undercooling driving force** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Undercooling = 29.28571 K**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **292.85714 J/mol**. At **Undercooling = 43.42857 K**, the response is **434.28571 J/mol**; within this idealization it increases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **10 J/mol per K**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nReference states, magnetic transitions, pressure, interfacial energy and kinetics can reverse simplistic interpretations of a free-energy diagram.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** Why is a thermodynamically favored product sometimes absent from a rapidly quenched specimen? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
