          "heading": "Lesson 1.3: Chemical potential and partitioning",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nIn multicomponent systems chemical potential is the partial molar Gibbs energy and expresses the cost of transferring an infinitesimal amount of a component. Two coexisting phases in full equilibrium have equal chemical potentials for every transferable species. Partition coefficients reflect phase compositions and depend on temperature and other alloying elements; they are not immutable constants. Mass conservation closes the equilibrium calculation.\n\n**Working relationship.** f_beta=(c0-c_alpha)/(c_beta-c_alpha) for a binary tie line on a consistent composition basis. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **chemical potential and partitioning**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nUse two fictional phase concentrations c_alpha=0.15 and c_beta=0.75 and bulk concentration c0=0.35 to calculate the two-phase fraction by the lever rule; cross-check reconstructed solute mass.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Lever-rule product fraction** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Bulk solute fraction = 0.4085714 fraction**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **0.43095238 fraction**. At **Bulk solute fraction = 0.4914286 fraction**, the response is **0.56904762 fraction**; within this idealization it increases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **1.6666667 fraction per fraction**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nComposition may be quoted by mole fraction, atom fraction, weight fraction or activity; substituting one for another without conversion can corrupt a tie-line balance.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** What would a solute map reveal about local equilibrium at a moving interface? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
