          "heading": "Lesson 1.4: Phase diagrams and invariant reactions",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nA binary phase diagram expresses equilibrium regions as functions of composition and temperature for a specified pressure. A tie line links compositions of phases in coexistence; the lever rule applies to fractions in an equilibrated two-phase region, not to arbitrary nonequilibrium micrographs. Eutectic liquid transforms to two solids, whereas eutectoid solid transforms to two other solids. Peritectic reactions involve liquid and one solid forming another solid.\n\n**Working relationship.** The lever rule is a mass-balance relation only at the temperature and phase compositions specified by a tie line. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **phase diagrams and invariant reactions**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nSketch an invented eutectic at 620 K with alpha and beta terminal solutions. Determine which compositions form primary crystals before the eutectic reaction, and explain the difference between primary phase fraction and final total alpha fraction.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Undercooling driving force** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Undercooling = 57.57143 K**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **575.71429 J/mol**. At **Undercooling = 71.71429 K**, the response is **717.14286 J/mol**; within this idealization it increases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **10 J/mol per K**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nEquilibrium diagrams do not encode cooling rate, undercooling, segregation or morphology; the same bulk composition can yield very different grain structures.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** What information must a casting engineer add before using an equilibrium diagram to predict dendrite spacing? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
