          "secNumber": "1.10",
          "heading": "Lesson 1.5: Driving forces along a thermal path",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nCooling or heating moves the system through regions of thermodynamic preference, but the driving force is a difference in Gibbs energies evaluated at the actual temperature and composition. Near a first-order equilibrium transition an entropy discontinuity leads to an approximate linear dependence on undercooling. Path, nucleation sites and composition gradients can create hysteresis between cooling and reheating. Thermodynamic reversibility does not imply microstructural reversibility.\n\n**Working relationship.** |Delta G_m|\u2248L_m (Delta T/T_eq), close to a first-order transition with approximately constant latent heat. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **driving forces along a thermal path**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nFor an idealized solid-solid transformation with transition temperature 1000 K and latent heat 10 kJ/mol, estimate 500 J/mol bulk driving force at 50 K undercooling using a near-transition linear approximation.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Lever-rule product fraction** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Bulk solute fraction = 0.5742857 fraction**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **0.70714286 fraction**. At **Bulk solute fraction = 0.6571429 fraction**, the response is **0.8452381 fraction**; within this idealization it increases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **1.6666667 fraction per fraction**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nThis linearization is poor far below the equilibrium point or when transformation enthalpy varies strongly with temperature; elastic and interfacial penalties may be comparable.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** What measurement distinguishes a free-energy driving force from an observed calorimetric onset? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
