          "secNumber": "1.11",
          "heading": "Integrated investigation: A metastable binary alloy",
          "content": "### Scientific problem and hypotheses\n\nA hypothetical alloy cools through a two-phase field but looks single phase under a low-resolution optical microscope. The laboratory must decide whether this reflects equilibrium solubility, a nucleation delay or insufficient spatial resolution. Define a primary observable and a rival mechanism. Hypothesis A should predict the observed conversion from the intended transformation; hypothesis B should explain a similar gross signal through a competing event. Name the observations needed to prefer one. A theory that can be made to fit every possible thermal or structural trace is not adequately discriminating. Describe a negative control, a second spatial sampling location, and an independent physical modality capable of challenging the initial phase identification.\n\n### Mathematical framework\n\nEstablish mass balance and phase identities using a chosen binary tie line; calculate Gibbs preference separately from interface penalties; outline a temperature\u2013time schedule and predict what diffraction and chemistry maps should show. Distinguish equilibrium state functions from kinetic rate parameters, then write the relevant conservation law. If a temperature is specified, record whether it is a furnace set point, actual specimen center, interface point or surface reading. If a phase fraction is inferred, identify the normalization and its endpoint calibration. Compare output at two values using the unit's interactive models; report the numerical change, dimension and range of acceptable inputs. Where a constitutive relation is approximate, propagate rather than conceal parameter uncertainty.\n\nA rigorous explanation should include what would happen at slow cooling compared with quenching, and explain the conditions under which both trajectories would arrive at similar or different microstructures. Include whether substitutional diffusion, interstitial transport, cooperative lattice change, or interface movement supplies the controlling timescale. If the sample contains gradients, explain how a local transformation result might differ between edge and core. The comparison should be phrased as testable predicted signatures, not a guessed dramatic micrograph.\n\n### Measurement and validation sequence\n\nRequire two solute maps at different scales and diffraction with a reference specimen; track thermal history and explicitly state uncertainty in composition and lattice assignment. First capture the untreated material's structure and chemistry. Then document time-temperature history using two relevant temperature locations. Finally collect phase-sensitive and morphology-sensitive data at several interrupted processing states. Specify whether each observation is destructive, whether thermal cycling alters the sample, and how replicate variance will be measured. Blind a second analyst to the proposed mechanism and ask them to calculate a phase fraction or kinetic parameter from the primary traces. Record disagreements before revising interpretation.\n\n### Claim boundaries and report requirements\n\nThe equilibrium diagram is a boundary condition, not a kinetic movie; a single end-state measurement cannot prove the route by which the state was reached. A polished final report must include: starting microstructure; phase definitions; a reaction or chemical-potential argument; a checked worked estimate; a temperature\u2013time diagram annotated with sensor location; synthetic model graphs clearly marked as simulated; a second-method validation plan; and a concise list of conditions that would make the engineering conclusion unsafe or invalid. Numerical compatibility is a necessary implementation check but is not sufficient evidence of mechanism or service suitability. Explain precisely what further data would turn the exercise into a candidate processing procedure.\n\n### Interact with this unit's models\n\n<!-- MODEL_0 -->\n\n<!-- MODEL_1 -->",
