          "heading": "Lesson 5.1: Austenite, ferrite and cementite",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nAustenite has face-centered-cubic iron-rich crystal structure, while ferrite is body-centered-cubic and cementite is an iron carbide with a distinct crystal structure. Carbon has substantially different solubilities in these constituents. Their coexistence and fractions depend on carbon composition and heat-treatment history. Thermodynamic phase fractions are not interchangeable with the proportions of microstructural features such as pearlite colonies.\n\n**Working relationship.** For a two-phase tie line, use phase compositions at one temperature and a consistent mass-fraction basis. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **austenite, ferrite and cementite**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nUse an approximate simplified eutectoid tie line with C_ferrite=0.022 wt%, C_austenite=0.76 wt% and C_cementite=6.67 wt%; explicitly determine which lever-rule question is meaningful just below the eutectoid boundary.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Steel idealized carbide mass fraction** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Overall carbon concentration = 0.2828571 wt%**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **0.039238439 fraction**. At **Overall carbon concentration = 0.5357143 wt%**, the response is **0.077273509 fraction**; within this idealization it increases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **0.15042118 fraction per wt%**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nReal steels include substitutional alloying elements and nonequilibrium products; equilibrium fractions alone cannot predict hardness or retained austenite.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** How can diffraction distinguish retained austenite from a ferrite-plus-carbide microstructure? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
