          "heading": "Lesson 5.2: Pearlite formation and interlamellar spacing",
          "content": "### Concept, boundary conditions and physical meaning\n\nPearlite is a lamellar mixture of ferrite and cementite produced by a diffusional eutectoid reaction. Its colony nucleation, interface migration and carbon redistribution control kinetics. Lower transformation temperature may refine spacing within a useful temperature range because more driving force supports thinner diffusion paths, but very low temperatures also slow carbon migration. Pearson-style generic strength estimates are not a substitute for measured pearlite spacing distributions.\n\n**Working relationship.** Eutectoid gamma\u2192alpha+Fe3C is a coupled diffusional transformation; its morphology is not itself a single phase. Read the terms in this relationship as quantities attached to a specific state, geometry or path. Do not assume that a convenient equation has proved the physical mechanism. For **pearlite formation and interlamellar spacing**, define the initial state and possible product before assigning a fraction, temperature or rate. A bulk average can conceal a sharp local concentration field, different lattice orientations or particles too small for the selected imaging method. When temperature or composition is not uniform, a single numerical parameter is at best a spatially weighted approximation; every reported number should state which part of the specimen it represents.\n\n### Guided experiment and a concrete numerical anchor\n\nMeasure 20 intercepts across invented pearlite colonies and infer an average lamellar period with uncertainty rather than drawing a single line on a photomicrograph.\n\nThe nearby calculator **Empirical martensite cooling fraction** offers one deliberately simplified numerical anchor, chosen to practice dimensional analysis rather than to represent the material in the vignette. Set **Steel temperature = 171.4286 \u00b0C**, with all other model values held as published: the response is **0.85974397 fraction**. At **Steel temperature = 207.1429 \u00b0C**, the response is **0.79225181 fraction**; within this idealization it decreases. The finite-difference sensitivity for these two settings is approximately **-0.0018897803 fraction per \u00b0C**. Check the direction analytically and consider whether the change would be distinguishable from your measurement resolution. Do not confuse this sensitivity with the effect of changing every correlated process variable in a real furnace.\n\n### Rival interpretations and evidence that could separate them\n\nOptical microscopy may not resolve fine lamellae; apparent colony boundaries and polishing artifacts contaminate automated segmentations.\n\n### Examination, synthesis and a failure test\n\n**Challenge question:** What controlled isothermal treatment could isolate spacing effects from prior-austenite grain size? In a full solution, draw or describe the initial phase boundary, list governing assumptions, and calculate the two synthetic responses shown above from the published formula. Interpret the sign of their difference physically rather than merely repeating the mathematical trend. Next propose two mutually discriminating outcomes of an experiment, each compatible with known conservation laws. Identify one uncertainty that could reverse your mechanistic conclusion and an instrument capable of measuring it.",
