          "content": "**Guiding question:** How can tiny secondary phases strengthen or degrade an alloy?\n\nA fictional precipitation-hardened alloy reaches its largest hardness at intermediate aging time but becomes vulnerable to one environmental exposure. A complete analysis separates the phase identities, the relevant conserved quantities, the imposed temperature\u2013time path, and the measurable response. It is not enough to state that 'a new phase formed': the experiment must establish what is new, where it appeared, whether composition changed and whether the rate is constrained by diffusion, interface kinetics or transformation strain. This unit emphasizes **particle-strength evolution**, using a **a supersaturated matrix and precipitates** to test plausible mechanisms instead of treating a suggestive curve as proof.\n\nUse a coarsening model to infer particle scale changes, compare bypass and cutting hypotheses, then define a constrained optimization across strength and corrosion. Before analyzing each lesson, prepare a ledger of observations, assumed boundary conditions and competing interpretations. Distinguish thermodynamic possibility from a nucleation pathway, a transformation rate and eventual property evolution. Include the thermal program and a practical measurement time resolution. Repeat a calculation at two values of a chosen input; the sensitivity often exposes whether the dominant uncertainty lies in material constants, microstructure or instrumentation.",
