          "content": "At fixed temperature and pressure, an equilibrium state minimizes Gibbs energy subject to mass balance. Keep energy per mole separate from energy per unit volume and per atom. Chemical potential equality governs components shared across phases; nominal alloy composition alone does not tell the composition of either phase. For binary tie-line computations use one consistent concentration basis. If phases have different densities and a question asks for volume fractions, convert from mass fractions with phase densities and renormalize. If temperature varies during the experiment, do not treat a free-energy difference at one temperature as the driving force for every point in the experiment. A driving force permits a process thermodynamically but does not guarantee a fast transition.",
